   "source": [
    "___COVARIANCE:\n",
    "-It only have direction and not magnitude.\n",
    "-It tells upto what intensity the data points is going to change.\n",
    "-It dosent tell are the points are connected or have any relation.\n",
    "___TYPES:\n",
    "1.Positive covariance- if the variables or poitns increase or decrease together.\n",
    "2.Negative covariance- if one variable increase while the other decrease.\n",
    "3.Zero covariance- if the variables do not have any relationship.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "031bcb65-07fc-47f5-84ca-b5b5dea38bb5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "___COVARIANCE vs CORRELATION:\n",
    "-Covariance measures the direction of the relationship but not the strength.\n",
    "-Correlation is a normalized version of covariance which measures both the strength and direction of the relationship and ranges between -1 to 1.\n",
    "-Covariance can be difficult to interpret as its magnitude depends on the scale of the variables, whereas Correlation is easier to understand since its normalized."
   ]
  }
 ],
